mentally ill and violent jaunty bravery  told his carers he wanted to hurt or  kill someone alone and unsupervised at  the Tate Modern Art Gallery he carried  out his threat he grabbed a six-year-old  boy threw him a hundred feet from a  viewing gantry and almost killed him  witnesses said he shrugged and laughed  at what he'd just done he told the boy's  father yes I am mad after seeing the  attacker jailed a detective read a  statement from the young boy's family  the acts committed by this individual  against our son is unspeakable  words cannot express the horror and the  fear that his actions have brought upon  us and our son he was now wondering why  is in hospital how can one explain to a  child that someone deliberately tried to  kill him from what the doctor said he  has many years of physiotherapy ahead of  him and we have no prospects or plan for  the future  other than being by his side the boy on  holiday from France suffered multiple  fractures and a bleed on his brain he is  still in a wheelchair today wear splints  on his left arm and both his legs and  spends his days in a corset molded his  waist sat in his wheelchair the nights  are always extremely difficult his sleep  is very agitated he is in pain he wakes  up many times and he cries jaunty  bravery suffers from autism spectrum  disorder a behavioural condition but the  judge said that did not explain his  attack on the boy mrs. Justice McGowan  told him the fear he must have  experienced and the horror his parents  felt are beyond imagination you had  intended to kill someone that day you  almost killed that six-year-old boy you  will spend the greater part if not all  of your life detained you may never be  released  social services had been supervising the  teenager since he was a juvenile he was  in supported accommodation but allowed  out alone for  for hours at a time the council said the  case was being reviewed and it would  learn from the findings  jaunty bravery is being treated at  Broadmoor secure Hospital his parents  said they could never forgive him but  felt they had been let down by the  system Martin Brant Sky News
 

